## Page 2: Component Deep-Dive & API Reference

### 2.1 Backend Components

#### ReAct Agent ([iac_agent.py](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/backend/app/iac_agent.py))

The core agent uses LangChain's `create_react_agent` with the `hwchase17/react` prompt template. It wraps the agent in an `AgentExecutor` with:
- **Max 8 iterations** to prevent infinite loops
- **Intermediate step tracking** for UI trace visualization
- **Error handling** for LLM parsing failures

#### Agent Tools ([agent_tools.py](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/backend/app/agent_tools.py))

| Tool | Purpose | Process |
|------|---------|---------|
| [generate_iac](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/backend/app/agent_tools.py#6-30) | Generate IaC from natural language | LLM generates raw code → auto-detects type (Terraform/K8s/Ansible/Docker) → writes to `iac_output/` |
| [check_policies](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/backend/app/agent_tools.py#32-70) | Validate generated IaC | Reads last generated file → LLM audits against 10 security/compliance checks → returns PASS/FAIL |
| [fix_iac](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/backend/app/agent_tools.py#72-108) | Auto-remediate violations | Reads original code + violations → LLM regenerates fixed version → overwrites in `iac_output/` |

#### IaC Type Detection ([detect_iac_type](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/backend/app/agent_tools.py#110-122))

```
Content contains "apiVersion" or "kind:"  →  Kubernetes
Content contains "docker-compose"          →  Docker Compose
Content contains "- name:" and "hosts:"    →  Ansible
Default                                    →  Terraform
```

#### Policy Engine ([policy_engine.py](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/backend/app/policy_engine.py)) — 465 lines

A comprehensive **rule-based static analysis engine** that evaluates IaC against organizational policies. It supports all four IaC types:

| IaC Type | Checks Performed |
|----------|-----------------|
| **Terraform (Plan JSON)** | EC2 instance types, security group rules (SSH/RDP open to world), S3 public ACLs, S3 encryption, RDS encryption & public access, IAM wildcard policies, EKS secrets encryption, Lambda tracing, OpenSearch encryption, resource tagging |
| **Terraform (HCL)** | Blocked instances, open CIDRs, public ACLs, storage encryption, deletion protection, tag presence |
| **Kubernetes** | Root user, privileged containers, privilege escalation, resource limits/requests, liveness/readiness probes, replica counts, image tag pinning, hostNetwork/hostPID, read-only filesystem, namespace |
| **Ansible** | become/root usage, plaintext passwords, shell/command overuse, no_log for secrets, ignore_errors, file permissions (777/666) |
| **Docker Compose** | Privileged mode, image pinning, sensitive port exposure, env secrets, memory/CPU limits, restart policy, sensitive host mounts, custom networks |
| **Universal** | Hardcoded secrets (6 patterns), IAM wildcard actions, blocked regions |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The policy engine produces two categories: **Violations** (hard fails) and **Warnings** (advisory). Only violations trigger a `FAIL` status.

#### LLM Abstraction Layer ([llm/](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/backend/app/llm/factory.py#8-22))

Uses the **Factory Pattern** to select the LLM provider at runtime via the `LLM_PROVIDER` environment variable:

```python
# .env
LLM_PROVIDER=azure  # Accepted values: gemini, azure, openai
```

All providers implement the [BaseLLM](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/backend/app/llm/base.py#5-10) abstract class with two key interfaces:
- `.generate(prompt)` — Direct text generation (used by tools)
- `.llm` — LangChain-compatible model instance (used by the ReAct agent)

| Provider | Model | Authentication |
|----------|-------|---------------|
| **Azure OpenAI** | `gpt-4o` | Azure AD/Entra ID (ClientSecretCredential) |
| **Google Gemini** | `gemini-2.5-flash` | API Key |
| **OpenAI** | `gpt-4o-mini` | API Key |

### 2.2 Frontend Components

#### Chat Application ([App.jsx](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/frontend/src/App.jsx))
- **Conversation memory**: Sends last 6 messages as context to the backend
- **Quick prompts**: 4 pre-built infrastructure templates for fast starts
- **Auto-fix flow**: On `FAIL` status, shows a "Regenerate & Fix IaC" button that calls `/fix-agent`
- **Session history**: [useHistory](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/frontend/src/hooks/useHistory.js#6-38) hook persists up to 50 runs in `localStorage`

#### Component Breakdown

| Component | Responsibility |
|-----------|---------------|
| [ChatMessage](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/frontend/src/components/ChatMessage.jsx#86-181) | Renders user/assistant messages, parses markdown code blocks with copy-to-clipboard, shows PASS/FAIL badge and fix button |
| `HistorySidebar` | Lists past runs, click to restore full conversation, delete individual entries |
| `StatusBadge` | Color-coded PASS (green) / FAIL (red) / UNKNOWN (gray) indicator |
| [StepTrace](file:///c:/Users/ritan/OneDrive/Desktop/Iac-agent/frontend/src/components/StepTrace.jsx#4-73) | Expandable trace of agent tool calls — shows tool name, input, output, and total duration |

### 2.3 API Endpoints

| Method | Endpoint | Request Body | Response |
|--------|----------|-------------|----------|
| `POST` | `/run-agent` | `{ prompt, conversation_history[] }` | `{ id, timestamp, prompt, result, steps[], total_steps, status, duration_ms }` |
| `POST` | `/fix-agent` | `{ prompt, conversation_history[] }` | Same as above |
| `GET` | `/history` | — | `[run, run, ...]` |
| `DELETE` | `/history` | — | `{ message }` |

### 2.4 End-to-End Request Flow

```
1. User types: "Create an S3 bucket with encryption"
                    │
2. Frontend sends POST /run-agent with prompt + conversation history
                    │
3. Backend builds context prompt (with memory if history exists)
                    │
4. ReAct Agent starts reasoning loop:
   │
   ├─ Thought: "I need to generate Terraform for an S3 bucket"
   ├─ Action:  generate_iac("S3 bucket with encryption")
   │   └─ LLM generates Terraform HCL → iac_writer saves to iac_output/main.tf
   │
   ├─ Thought: "Now I should validate this against policies"
   ├─ Action:  check_policies("")
   │   └─ LLM audits the code → Returns STATUS: PASS/FAIL with violations
   │
   └─ Final Answer: Summary + code blocks + policy status
                    │
5. Backend extracts code blocks, detects status, builds response
                    │
6. Frontend renders: message bubble + code blocks + PASS/FAIL badge
                    │
7. If FAIL → User clicks "Regenerate & Fix" → POST /fix-agent
   │
   └─ Agent calls fix_iac(violations) → check_policies() → returns fixed code
```

### 2.5 Configuration

```env
# Provider Selection
LLM_PROVIDER=azure              # gemini | azure | openai

# Google Gemini
GEMINI_API_KEY=your_key

# Azure OpenAI (Entra ID)
AZURE_TENANT_ID=...
AZURE_CLIENT_ID=...
AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=...
AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=https://your-resource.openai.azure.com/
AZURE_OPENAI_API_VERSION=2025-01-01-preview
AZURE_OPENAI_CHATGPT_DEPLOYMENT=gpt-4o

# OpenAI
OPENAI_API_KEY=your_key
```

### 2.6 Running the Project

```bash
# Backend
cd Iac-agent
pip install -r requirements.txt
uvicorn backend.app.main:app --reload --port 8000

# Frontend
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev    # → http://localhost:5173
```

